The  utility  is  used to configure network bridge parameters and
retrieve network bridge parameters and status  from  the  kernel.
The  bridging  function  is implemented by the driver.  A network
bridge creates a logical link between two or more IEEE  802  net‐
works  that  use the same (or framing format.  For example, it is
possible to bridge Ethernet and 802.11 networks together, but  it
is  not  possible  to  bridge  Ethernet  and Token Ring together.
Bridge interfaces are created using  the  command’s  sub‐command.
All other bridge configuration is performed using The options are
as  follows:  Display the status of all bridge devices present on
the system.  This flag is mutually exclusive with all other  sub‐
commands.   All  other operations require that a bridge be speci‐
fied.  If a bridge is specified with no sub‐commands, the  status
of  that  bridge  is  displayed.   The following sub‐commands are
available: Start forwarding packets on the bridge.  Stop forward‐
ing packets on the bridge.  Add the interface named by as a  mem‐
ber of the bridge.  The interface is put into promiscuous mode so
that it can receive every packet sent on the network.  Remove the
interface named by from the bridge.  Promiscuous mode is disabled
on the interface when it is removed from the bridge.  Display the
addresses  that have been learned by the bridge.  Set the size of
the bridge address cache to The default is 100 entries.  Set  the
timeout  of  address  cache entries to seconds.  If is zero, then
address cache entries will not be expired.  The default  is  1200
seconds.  Delete from the address cache.  Delete all dynamically‐
learned  addresses from the address cache.  Delete all addresses,
including static addresses, from the address cache.  Mark an  in‐
terface as a interface.  When the bridge has no address cache en‐
try  (either  dynamic or static) for the destination address of a
packet, the bridge will forward the packet to all  member  inter‐
faces marked as This is the default for all interfaces added to a
bridge.   Clear the attribute on a member interface.  For packets
without the attribute, the only packets forwarded on  the  inter‐
face are broadcast or multicast packets and packets for which the
destination  address  is  known to be on the interface’s segment.
Enable packet filtering with on the bridge.  The  current  imple‐
mentation  passes  all  ARP  and  RARP packets through the bridge
while filtering IP and IPv6 packets through the configured packet
